---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTASection from '@/components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Iranian Apple Export: A Practical Guide for International Buyers | SibExport"
  description="How to buy apples from Iran: origins in West Azerbaijan, red, golden and green categories, specifications, pricing factors, documentation and the first-order process."
  path="/blog/iranian-apple-export-guide/"
  ogImage="/images/blog-guide.png"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Blog & Export Insights', href: '/blog/' },
      { label: 'Iranian Apple Export Guide' },
    ]}
  />

  <article class="container-site py-14 lg:py-20">
    <header class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">Buying Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">
        Iranian Apple Export: A Practical Guide for International Buyers
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        By the SibExport team · Urmia, West Azerbaijan, Iran
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-site mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        Iran is one of the world's largest apple producers, yet for many
        international buyers it remains an unfamiliar origin compared with
        Poland, Turkey or China. That unfamiliarity is often costly: buyers
        who overlook Iran miss a large crop, competitive pricing and — for
        markets in the Middle East, Caucasus and Central Asia — transit
        times their usual origins cannot match. This guide walks through
        what an importer actually needs to know to buy Iranian apples
        competently: where the fruit grows, what the commercial categories
        are, how specifications and pricing work, and how a first order
        moves from inquiry to delivered cargo.
      </p>

      <h2>Where Iranian apples are grown</h2>

<figure class="my-8">
      <img
        src="/images/blog-guide.svg"
        alt="Guide to importing apples from Iran"
        width="1200"
        height="480"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full rounded-xl border border-border shadow-sm"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-muted-foreground">Guide to importing apples from Iran</figcaption>
    </figure>
      <p>
        Iran's apple production is concentrated in the country's northwest,
        above all in West Azerbaijan province. The city of Urmia, at the
        center of the region, sits on a high plateau whose climate is close
        to ideal for apple growing: winters cold enough for proper
        dormancy, summers warm enough for growth, and autumn days long and
        sunny enough to develop the sugars and color that export fruit
        needs. East Azerbaijan, and other northwestern areas, add to the
        regional crop.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical consequence for buyers is origin concentration.
        Because so much of the national crop grows within a compact
       , well-connected area, a supplier based in Urmia can physically
        follow the fruit — from the orchard it is picked in, through the
        packing facility, to the truck at loading. Buyers coming from more
        fragmented origins often find this oversight is the single biggest
        difference in working with Iranian supply.
      </p>

      <h2>The commercial categories: red, golden and green</h2>
      <p>
        Wholesale apple trade is organized around commercial categories
        rather than botanical variety lists, and Iranian supply divides
        naturally into three classes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Red apples</strong> — the volume leader, prized for color,
          firmness and versatile sizing. Most first-time buyers start here.
          See our page on <a href="/red-apple-export/">red apple export from
          Iran</a> for the full category profile.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Golden apples</strong> — the sweet, pale-gold category with
          strong recognition in Middle Eastern markets. Thinner skin makes
          handling quality decisive. Details on
          <a href="/golden-apple-export/">golden apple export</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Green apples</strong> — the tart, firm complement that
          completes a three-color program. Their robustness suits demanding
          distribution. Details on <a href="/green-apple-export/">green apple
          supply</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Within each category, the specific varieties available in a given
        season change with harvest and storage cycles. A reliable supplier
        confirms variety availability per order in writing — be wary of any
        origin website that promises an unchanging variety list.
      </p>

      <h2>Specifications: the document that decides everything</h2>
      <p>
        The most common failure in international fruit trade is not bad
        fruit — it is mismatched expectations. The buyer expected one color
        standard; the supplier shipped to another. The fix is procedural,
        not heroic: a <strong>written specification sheet</strong> agreed
        before sourcing begins, covering:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Category and variety, as confirmed available for the season</li>
        <li>Size, expressed as count per export carton</li>
        <li>Grade: tolerances for color, firmness and surface defects</li>
        <li>Packing: carton specification, interior packaging, pallet plan</li>
        <li>Labeling and marking, per destination requirements</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every subsequent step — grading, inspection, loading — is checked
        against that sheet. Our own approach to specifications is described
        in detail under
        <a href="/apple-quality-specifications/">apple quality and
        specifications</a>, and packaging considerations under
        <a href="/apple-packaging/">apple packaging for export</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What drives wholesale apple prices</h2>
      <p>
        Buyers researching <em>Iranian apple export prices and
        specifications</em> will search in vain for a meaningful published
        price list — and the absence is informative. Wholesale apple
        pricing depends on interacting factors that shift through the
        season:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Category and variety</strong> within the season's crop</li>
        <li><strong>Size count</strong> — larger presentation fruit carries a premium</li>
        <li><strong>Grade strictness</strong> — tighter tolerances cost more to produce</li>
        <li><strong>Season position</strong> — fresh-harvest, early-storage and late-season fruit are different markets</li>
        <li><strong>Volume</strong> — program volumes price differently from single-truck orders</li>
        <li><strong>Packing</strong> — carton spec and retail-ready formats affect cost</li>
        <li><strong>Delivery terms</strong> — Incoterms decide which logistics costs sit where</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A quotation prepared without stating these factors is a number that
        will not survive contact with the real order. The useful discipline
        for buyers: always compare offers on identical specification and
        terms, never on headline price alone.
      </p>

      <h2>Documentation for an Iranian apple shipment</h2>
      <p>
        Fresh fruit lives and dies on paperwork. The documents commonly
        involved in an Iranian apple export include the commercial invoice,
        packing list, certificate of origin and phytosanitary certificate —
        with destination-specific additions varying by country and
        regulation. Two practical notes for buyers:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Documentation requirements are destination-specific. Tell your
          supplier the importing country early so the document set is
          prepared correctly from the start, not corrected at the border.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask to review document copies before dispatch. A supplier who
          coordinates documents professionally will share them without
          hesitation.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Iranian apple export documentation requirements are a frequent
        source of anxiety for first-time buyers; in practice they are a
        checklist, and a competent supplier manages the checklist with you.
      </p>

      <h2>Destinations: where Iranian apples fit</h2>
      <p>
        Iran's position puts it within road-transport distance of several
        major apple-importing economies. Markets commonly served from the
        northwest include Iraq, the United Arab Emirates, Russia, Armenia,
        Azerbaijan and Central Asian countries — each with its own demand
        profile and regulatory requirements. Shorter transit is not merely
        cheaper; fruit that spends days rather than weeks in transit
        arrives firmer with more remaining shelf life. Our assessment of
        each market is on the <a href="/export-destinations/">export
        destinations</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>How a first order proceeds</h2>
      <p>
        The path from inquiry to delivered cargo is a defined workflow, not
        improvisation. In summary:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Inquiry</strong> — you share category, rough volume and destination</li>
        <li><strong>Specification</strong> — details are refined into a written sheet</li>
        <li><strong>Availability</strong> — the supplier confirms what the season can genuinely deliver</li>
        <li><strong>Quotation</strong> — a written offer tied to the specification</li>
        <li><strong>Agreement</strong> — terms, payment and obligations confirmed in writing</li>
        <li><strong>Quality &amp; packaging confirmation</strong> — the spec sheet is finalized</li>
        <li><strong>Documentation</strong> — certificates and paperwork prepared</li>
        <li><strong>Transport</strong> — routing and cold-chain arranged</li>
        <li><strong>Shipment</strong> — loading under supervision, dispatch, tracked delivery</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Each step is explained in full on the
        <a href="/apple-export-process/">apple export process</a> page. Many
        buyers wisely begin with a single truck or container to test their
        market — a sensible first step any good supplier will support.
      </p>

      <h2>Choosing a supplier in Iran: what to look for</h2>
      <p>
        Because this guide is practical, it should say plainly what
        separates a reliable Iranian apple supplier from a disappointing
        one:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Written specifications</strong>, offered without being asked</li>
        <li><strong>Honest availability answers</strong> — including "not this season"</li>
        <li><strong>Origin proximity</strong> — a real operating presence near the orchards</li>
        <li><strong>Inspection discipline</strong> — defined check points, and welcome for third-party inspectors</li>
        <li><strong>Clear English communication</strong> on WhatsApp, phone and email</li>
        <li><strong>Document competence</strong> — copies shared before dispatch</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        SibExport was built around exactly these practices. If you are
        evaluating Iranian supply for your market, the fastest way to test
        us is to <a href="/request-a-quotation/">request a quotation</a> with
        a real requirement and judge the quality of the answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions from first-time buyers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Is there a minimum order?</strong> Minimums depend on
        category, packing and destination, and are confirmed with your
        quotation. Many buyers start with a single truck load.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Can I visit before ordering?</strong> Buyers are welcome to
        arrange visits and third-party inspection at agreed stages — any
        confident supplier will say yes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Which apple category should I start with?</strong> Red
        apples are the broadest volume category; golden and green complete
        a full-range program. Your market's demand profile decides — our
        <a href="/iranian-apple-varieties/">Iranian apple varieties</a> page
        helps you choose.
      </p>
      <p>
        More answers are on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>Final word</h2>
      <p>
        Iranian apples are not a niche alternative — they are a major,
        established origin whose practical advantages are proximity, crop
        quality and price competitiveness in regional trade. The buyers who
        succeed with Iranian supply are the ones who buy the way
        professionals do: written specifications, verified quality points,
        honest schedules. Whether you buy from SibExport or elsewhere, buy
        that way. And when you are ready to test the origin, we are one
        WhatsApp message away.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection
    title="Ready to buy Iranian apples?"
    description="Send your requirement through the quotation form or message us on WhatsApp — and judge our answer for yourself."
  />
</BaseLayout>
